%erg 0.1
Title: appendLogLine places a log entry after the terminal blank and erg check does not see it
Created: 2026-10-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-03T12:04Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`erg new` writes a log section whose entry run is terminated by a blank line
before `--- body ---`. `appendLogLine` (src/go/close.go) then inserts the new
entry directly before `\\n` + `--- body ---`, i.e. AFTER that terminal blank:

    created
                <- terminal blank (end of entry run)
    new entry           <- lands here, out of the entry run
    --- body ---

The new entry is displaced past the blank that terminates the entry run, and
the terminal blank is consumed. Since `erg log`, `erg close`, `erg label` and
`erg unlabel` all route through appendLogLine, the tool's own systematic
output produces this shape: in the maintainer's harness corpus 460 of 534
ticket files carry it. A validator that never looks at position reports PASS
either way, so nothing distinguishes a well-placed entry from one placed where
the validator does not read (harness ticket 0879).

A second blind spot: log timestamps are never checked for ordering. A wave
rebase legitimately replays older-stamped lines after newer ones (65 corpus
files), so ordering must be advisory, never a rejection.

## Scope

- appendLogLine: normalize-then-append. The entry is inserted at the end of
  the entry run (contiguous with previous entries), before the blank run that
  precedes `--- body ---`; a missing terminal blank is restored. No write
  refusal anywhere: the displaced shape is the tool's own systematic output,
  and refusing would wedge every corpus that carries it (callers include
  close, label, unlabel, log and the adopter's CI validate-tickets gate).
- erg check: advisory WARN (exit 0) when a log-format line sits after the
  entry run's last blank (displaced entry), and advisory WARN when log
  timestamps decrease within the entry run (routine rebase replay is exempt
  from rejection, reported only).

## Test

- Red: TestAppendLogLine case on content ending `created\\n\\n--- body ---`
  must yield the new entry contiguous with the previous entries and the
  terminal blank preserved (`created\\n<line>\\n\\n--- body ---`). The current
  code fails it.
- New corpus warnings fire on a displaced entry and on regressive timestamps;
  a well-formed file draws neither; exit code stays 0 in all cases.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] appendLogLine keeps entries contiguous and preserves/restores the
      terminal blank; no caller refusal added
- [ ] erg check warns (advisory, exit 0) on a displaced log entry
- [ ] erg check warns (advisory, exit 0) on regressive log timestamps
- [ ] make check passes
